                <pb facs="0803" n="517" sameAs="frus:frus1916Supp/pg517"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 763.72112/2991</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Consul General at London</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Skinner</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">London</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1916-09-23">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">September 23, 1916</hi>
                        </date>.<lb/>[<hi rendition="#italic">Received 10.50 a. m.</hi>]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>Department’s August 5.<anchor n="1" xml:id="d640-f1"/> Agreement to give free
                    transit to American tobacco paid for August 4 construed by Foreign Office to
                    mean paid for by European importer. This decision injuriously affects shippers
                    who bought for export to selves or European buying houses which they control.
                    These interests urgently desire broader construction of expression “paid for,”
                    especially as to goods afloat.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Skinner</hi>
                        
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
